# Production deployment environment for the university VPS.
#
# Install as a root-owned file OUTSIDE the repository:
#   install -o root -g root -m 0600 .env.production \
#     /srv/aau/production/deploy/.env.production
#
# This file is the only place real production secrets live on the host. It is
# never committed, never copied into an image, and never passed as a build arg.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  QUOTING CONTRACT — read this before generating a password
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# `docker compose --env-file` does NOT read this file literally. Measured
# behaviour (tests/deploy/env-contract-drill.sh proves each of these):
#
#   PASS=a$B          the container receives `a` + the value of B  (mangled)
#   PASS=a${B}c       same                                          (mangled)
#   PASS=abc #x       the container receives `abc`                  (truncated)
#   PASS="a\nb"       the container receives a real newline         (mangled)
#   PASS="a$B"        interpolated even inside double quotes        (mangled)
#   PASS='a$B #x!'    the container receives `a$B #x!` exactly      (CORRECT)
#
# So the rule is simple and absolute:
#
#   >>> SINGLE-QUOTE EVERY VALUE. <<<
#
# A single-quoted value is passed through byte for byte, which is the only way
# a strong random password survives. Two consequences:
#
#   * A value must not contain a single quote (`'`). It would close the quote.
#     Generate passwords from an alphabet that excludes it, e.g.
#       openssl rand -base64 48 | tr -d "'\n"
#   * The deploy scripts read this file with deploy/scripts/read-env.sh, which
#     reproduces exactly these rules and REFUSES any line whose meaning would
#     depend on interpolation. A mis-quoted value fails the deploy loudly
#     instead of being used inconsistently by the scripts and the containers.

# ─── Release identity ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT: there is no APP_IMAGE here.
#
# The release-state file `/srv/aau/production/deploy/deployed-image` is the
# single source of truth for what is deployed, written only by a successful
# deploy or restore. An APP_IMAGE in this file would be configuration
# outliving the release it described: months later a bare `docker compose up`
# would silently start whatever digest was written here. The compose file has
# no default either, so such a call fails loudly.
#
# Use `deploy/scripts/compose.sh`, which supplies the digest from that file.

# ─── PostgreSQL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cluster administration role. Created as a server superuser by the official
# image. NEVER given to app-prod.
POSTGRES_ADMIN_USER='aau_admin'
POSTGRES_ADMIN_PASSWORD='REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_RANDOM_PASSWORD'
POSTGRES_DB='aau_platform'

# Non-superuser application role, provisioned on first init by
# deploy/postgres/initdb/10-application-role.sh. This is what Payload uses.
#
# The password appears exactly ONCE. The application assembles its connection
# URI from these discrete values and percent-encodes them itself
# (src/lib/database-url.ts), so a password containing `@`, `:`, `/`, `?`, `#`
# or `%` is handled correctly with no hand-encoding — and there is no second
# copy inside a URI that can drift out of step with this one.
APP_DB_USER='aau_app'
APP_DB_PASSWORD='REPLACE_WITH_A_DIFFERENT_STRONG_RANDOM_PASSWORD'

# ─── Redis ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Redis has no route off the host and no published port, but the Docker HOST
# can still reach it — `internal: true` is not host isolation. An
# unauthenticated Redis reachable from the host is a well-known escalation
# path, so it requires a password here as defence in depth.
#
# Any character is safe here except a single quote (the env-file contract: only
# single-quoted values are fully literal to `docker compose --env-file`). In
# particular '/', '#', '?', '%' and '@' are fine: the application receives the
# password as a discrete value and never has it interpolated into a redis://
# URI, where those characters are structural. Do not weaken a generated
# password to make it "URL-safe".
REDIS_PASSWORD='REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_RANDOM_PASSWORD'

# ─── Payload ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48 | tr -d "'\n"
# Must be stable across ordinary deployments — rotating it invalidates every
# existing session and is a deliberate security operation, not a release step.
PAYLOAD_SECRET='REPLACE_WITH_AT_LEAST_32_RANDOM_CHARACTERS'

# ─── First Super Admin ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Stable bootstrap identity. Runtime bootstrap creates this account if it does
# not exist and otherwise only validates it — it never resets the password.
#
# PRODUCTION NORMALLY NEEDS NO PASSWORD HERE AT ALL. Production's dataset is
# installed once from a verified platform backup
# (deploy/scripts/initialize-from-backup.sh), and that dataset already contains
# the university's real users including the real Super Admin. Set
# INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAIL to THAT account's address and leave INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD
# unset: the bootstrap then validates the existing account instead of creating
# one, and no migrated password is ever replaced.
#
# The initialization script checks this before anything is deployed and refuses
# when the configured address is absent from the restored dataset — because the
# bootstrap would otherwise CREATE an administrator the accepted dataset never
# had.
INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAIL='admin@aau.edu.example'
# Required ONLY when the configured account does not exist yet — that is, an
# empty first install, not an installation founded from a platform backup. Must
# be >= 12 characters with lowercase, uppercase, digit and special character.
#
# RETIRING IT IS TWO STEPS, NOT ONE. Deleting this line does not remove the
# variable from the already-running container's environment — the container
# keeps the value it was created with. After the first successful bootstrap:
#   1. delete this line;
#   2. deploy/scripts/compose.sh up --detach --force-recreate app-prod
#   3. verify /api/health is 200 and admin login still works.
# See "Retiring the bootstrap password" in deploy/README.md.
INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD='REPLACE_FOR_FIRST_LAUNCH_THEN_REMOVE'

# ─── Public form rate limits ──────────────────────────────────────────────
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS='900'
RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS='5'

# ─── Resource limits (OPTIONAL — override only from measurement) ──────────
# Left unset here on purpose, but NOT unlimited: the compose file now carries
# bounded production defaults (app 1280m, postgres 1024m, redis 256m, pids 512).
#
# Unlimited was the wrong default on this host. It runs cPanel, Exim, Dovecot,
# PowerDNS, Apache and MariaDB alongside this stack, on ~11 GiB with NO SWAP.
# An unbounded container that leaks does not fail locally — the kernel OOM
# killer scores every process on the host and may well choose MariaDB or
# Apache, taking the university's other sites down with it. Bounded limits keep
# the failure inside this stack, where the restart policy handles it.
#
# Override these once measured on the real host. Remember that a cutover runs
# app-candidate ALONGSIDE app-prod, so the app limit is paid twice at peak.
#   APP_MEM_LIMIT / APP_PIDS_LIMIT
#   POSTGRES_MEM_LIMIT / POSTGRES_PIDS_LIMIT
#   REDIS_MEM_LIMIT / REDIS_PIDS_LIMIT

# ─── Deliberately NOT set in production ───────────────────────────────────
# APP_IMAGE               — see "Release identity" above.
# DATABASE_URI            — superseded by the discrete DATABASE_* values the
# DATABASE_DIRECT_URI       compose file derives from APP_DB_* above. Setting
#                           either would override them and reintroduce the
#                           duplicated, hand-encoded password.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SERVER_URL  — inlined at `next build`; it is a CI build argument
#                           for the release image, not a runtime variable.
#                           Setting it here would have no effect.
# PAYLOAD_SERVER_ORIGIN   — only used by the disabled /next/seed route.
# PAYLOAD_SEED_SECRET     — only used by the disabled /next/seed route.
# PAYLOAD_DB_PUSH         — pinned to 'false' in the compose file.
# PAYLOAD_ALLOW_INSECURE_ORIGIN — test-only escape hatch. Setting it in
#                           production would disable the HTTPS-origin guard.
# S3_*                    — this VPS uses local persistent uploads.
